---
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Design" description="The design decisions behind cn-variants and its deliberately small API.">
  <header class="page-header">
    <p class="eyebrow">Design</p>
    <h1>Restraint is part of the API.</h1>
    <p class="page-summary">
      cn-variants exists for components that need typed class variants without adopting a component
      styling language.
    </p>
  </header>

  <article class="prose-narrow">
    <ol class="decision-list">
      <li>
        <h2>A function, not a configuration system</h2>
        <p>
          A lookup function narrows its argument to known keys, works with Tailwind IntelliSense,
          and returns the plain string every class-merging utility already understands.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h2>One axis at a time</h2>
        <p>
          Tone, size, and state stay as separate functions. Components decide how to combine them,
          so there is no named-argument convention or hidden precedence model to learn.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h2>Defaults belong to components</h2>
        <p>
          Prop destructuring already expresses defaults at the boundary where they matter. Moving
          them into a variant definition would create a second source of truth.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h2>Compound styles are conditions</h2>
        <p>
          JavaScript can already say “primary and large.” Keeping the expression in{" "}
          <code>cn()</code> gives TypeScript, refactors, and debuggers the full picture.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h2>Invalid runtime keys fail soft</h2>
        <p>
          TypeScript rejects invalid keys during development. If types are bypassed, an empty class
          string is safer than crashing a render over a styling concern.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h2>Options are a snapshot</h2>
        <p>
          The exposed <code>.options</code> object is frozen and detached from the caller's map. It
          supports inspection and type extraction without taking ownership of user data.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h2>Convenience dependencies stay direct</h2>
        <p>
          Asking every consumer to install and coordinate clsx and tailwind-merge would undermine
          the point of a tiny convenience package. Bundlers handle deduplication.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h2>Unused code should disappear</h2>
        <p>
          <code>cn</code> and <code>variants</code> live in separate modules and the package is marked
          side-effect free. Importing only the typed lookup does not need to retain the class merger.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </article>
</Layout>
